Reproduced. The race is exactly at `tally.go:36` (`counts[tok]++`): every per-shard goroutine concurrently reads and writes the single shared `counts` map.

This single bug explains **all three symptoms**:
- **Different totals per run** — `counts[tok]++` is read-modify-write; two goroutines that read the same value both write `value+1`, dropping one increment. How many drops is timing-dependent.
- **Process dies mid-aggregation ~1/10 runs** — Go's runtime detects truly-concurrent map writes and issues `fatal error: concurrent map writes`, which is unrecoverable (not a panic), so the whole ingest process just dies.
- **Single-shard never misbehaves** — one goroutine means no concurrency, hence no race.

The fix keeps the parallel-per-shard design: each goroutine tallies into its **own** local map (the expensive tokenize/tally work stays fully parallel), then merges them **serially** after `wg.Wait()`. Only the cheap merge is serialized — no lock contention on the hot path, throughput preserved.
